[start related work 1]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Macaluso et al., 2017-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b3cfc02cac9b750070c5469e65b890259c2934ac)
- venue: Frontiers in Physiology
- citation count: 24
[method]
The paper used a rapid reaching task in microgravity and neutral buoyancy to characterize focal reaching and postural control. Participants reached with the right index finger to targets on a tablet touchscreen while restrained with foot straps and stabilizing with the left hand on the table edge. Kinematic data were processed to compute reaction time, movement duration, endpoint mean tangential velocity, upper-limb displacement, success rate, index final deviation, temporal organization of arm angular elevation, peak angular velocity, relative angular deceleration duration, peak angular acceleration, time to peak angular acceleration, trunk displacement, and shoulder/hip forward displacement. This method is directly related to the proposed taikonaut study because it uses the same microgravity reaching paradigm and the same feedforward and postural kinematic indices that the research plan will use to distinguish conservative control from body-mass underestimation. The paper headings do not indicate use of the proposed two-joint arm model, movement utility theory, forward optimal controller, or submovement decomposition; those are extensions in the proposed plan.
[results]
No numerical values, effect sizes, or p-values are available in the provided paper section headings, so quantitative outcomes cannot be reported. The headings indicate that the paper evaluated learning effects, upper-limb displacement, accuracy, reaction time, movement duration and velocity, angular timing, velocity and acceleration profiles, and postural displacement. The discussion headings suggest the reported pattern was that reaching accuracy remained within normogravity standards, microgravity changed the timing of focal movement in a way consistent with planned conservative control, and postural displacement was reduced or reorganized; neutral buoyancy showed analogous behavior. These are qualitative inferences from the section headings, not extracted quantitative results.
[context]
The experimental context included microgravity and neutral buoyancy underwater conditions, with likely normogravity or ground comparisons. The proposed method specifies twelve taikonauts, pre/in/post-flight phases, an age-matched ground control group, three 12-cm targets at 45 degrees, 90 degrees, and 135 degrees, 120 pseudorandom trials, a 650-ms time constraint, auditory beeps in half trials, foot straps, left-hand stabilization, 100-Hz tablet sampling, repeated-measures ANOVA, and submovement decomposition. The paper excerpt lacks participant numbers, target geometry, sampling rate, and statistical details, so those reproducibility details cannot be confirmed from the provided text.
[end related work 1]

[start related work 2]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Thonnard et al., 2020-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/576fedf707528768a0d83b8bec20672d07f495ea)
- venue: No Venue
- citation count: 2
[method]
The paper describes GRIP parabolic-flight and ISS preparation protocols for controlled arm manipulation in weightlessness. In the seated dynamics protocol, participants first estimated the static coefficient of friction by active back-and-forth finger movements on a six-axis force sensor manipulandum, with the coefficient computed as the ratio of tangential to normal force at slip onset. They then performed oscillatory arm movements with three manipulandum masses at three frequencies, followed by point-to-point movements to visual targets presented in random order in vertical and horizontal directions, and repeated the friction test. In the seated references protocol, participants performed rapid discrete movements between two vertical targets with eyes open, then to remembered vertical targets with eyes closed, then analogous horizontal movements with eyes open and closed, and finally rapid upward and downward tapping collisions cued by audible tones, with friction tests at the start and end. A supine version repeated the seated references trials. These methods relate to the proposed method because both use repeated pre-, in-, and post-flight sessions, rapid finger or arm movements toward targets in microgravity, and physical restraint or stabilization to isolate limb kinematics; however, the paper uses a force-sensor manipulandum and parabolic-flight or ISS GRIP hardware rather than a tablet touchscreen, and it does not implement the proposed anisotropic-inertia target directions, two-joint arm modeling, movement utility theory, forward optimal controller, or submovement decomposition.
[results]
The provided sections report no quantitative movement-duration, peak-speed, peak-acceleration, reaction-time, or submovement outcomes for the seated or supine protocols. The only reported experimental outcome is from restraint-system preliminary tests: after testing restraint concepts in sitting and supine postures during vertical and horizontal oscillations and vertical collisions with different feet positions, pelvis restraint was strongly recommended and shoulder straps were important, and the weightlessness environment was required to evaluate the impact of parasitic body movements on subject stability. No statistical comparisons, baseline differences, or significance values are given.
[context]
The work was conducted during 21 parabolic flight campaigns between 1999 and 2014, including the 53rd ESA campaign in October 2010, and was used to prepare the GRIP experiment for the ISS. The GRIP equipment had Engineering, Science, Ground, and Flight models with similar hardware, including a chair box that served as a transport box and seated restraint, back and foot support, restraint belts, a manipulandum, a wrist box, and a six-axis force sensor. Sessions were designed for pre-, in-, and post-flight use. The proposed method similarly requires stable in-flight conditions, using foot straps and left-hand tabletop stabilization for twelve taikonauts, but differs by using a 100 Hz tablet touchscreen, three target directions at 45, 90, and 135 degrees, 120 pseudorandom trials, a 650 ms time constraint, an age-matched ground control group, and model-based analyses of effective limb mass and corrective submovements.
[end related work 2]

