- citation count: 1
[method]
The closest related method in the paper was a within-subjects touchscreen multidirectional tapping task used during a 60-day head-down tilt bed rest analog of microgravity. Participants tapped 11 targets arranged in a circle on a tablet; after each selection, the opposite target was highlighted, producing rotating two-dimensional pointing movements. Each session contained 16 blocks of 11 trials, with one fixed distance-width combination per block. Four target distances and four target widths produced 16 difficulty combinations, with index of difficulty ranging from 1.17 to 4.95. Measurements were taken before, during, and after head-down tilt: baseline 12 days before tilt, days 5, 21, and 56 of tilt, and recovery days 4 and 90. Participants lay supine, used only their dominant hand, and kept their elbow on a pad while the tablet was fixed on the bed. Performance was summarized by throughput, movement time, error count, and touch duration. Block-level aggregates were analyzed with linear mixed effects models for throughput, movement time, and touch duration, and with a zero-inflated Poisson generalized linear mixed model for error count, using time of measurement as a fixed effect and random intercepts for participant, target difficulty item, and their interaction. This method relates to the proposed taikonaut study because it also examines touchscreen target acquisition across pre, during, and post simulated microgravity phases, but it is an Earth-based head-down tilt analog rather than direct spaceflight data, and it does not test anisotropic reaching directions, two-joint arm mass perception, submovement decomposition, or peak acceleration timing.
[results]
Time of measurement significantly affected all four performance indicators: throughput, F(5, 1675.2) = 45.16, p < 0.001; movement time, F(5, 1605.81) = 72.465, p < 0.001; error count, chi-square(5) = 15.10, p < 0.01; and touch duration, F(5, 1631.26) = 14.15, p < 0.001. Throughput increased over the study period, with estimated increases of about 0.49 bits/s relative to baseline and about 0.95 bits/s at 90 days after head-down tilt. Movement time decreased significantly from baseline and early head-down tilt to later measurements, with no further substantial change after day 21 of head-down tilt. Error count was higher during head-down tilt conditions than at baseline, but recovery conditions did not differ significantly from baseline. Touch duration was elevated during head-down tilt days 5, 21, and 56 compared with baseline, and recovery conditions were lower than head-down tilt conditions in several comparisons. The relationship between movement time and index of difficulty was highly stable across head-down tilt and non-head-down tilt conditions, with a maximum difference in R-squared of 0.01. Overall, speed-based performance improved over time, likely due to practice, while error count and touch duration showed signs of transition-related degradation during head-down tilt. No clear early post-head-down tilt performance drop was detected at recovery day 4 or day 90.
[context]
The study involved 24 healthy adults aged 24 to 55 years, with a mean age of 33.33 years and standard deviation of 9.38 years; 16 were male and 8 were female. Participants reported high familiarity with touch devices, with a mean of 6.25 on a 7-point scale. The head-down tilt protocol lasted 60 days at a -6 degree head-down angle, and participants were not allowed to sit up or stand. The study was conducted in two successive cohorts of 12 participants. All task measurements were performed while lying down, with the tablet positioned on the bed and the participant using only the dominant hand. The tablet position was fixed from the baseline measurement and reproduced as accurately as possible. Data from recovery day 4 were available for only 12 participants due to data corruption. There was no ground-based control group, so practice effects could not be fully separated from physiological effects. The task measured aggregate touchscreen tapping performance rather than rapid 12 cm reaches at 45, 90, and 135 degrees, and the paper did not report 100 Hz finger position recording, foot straps, left-hand stabilization, submovement decomposition, or model-based tests of limb mass underestimation. This context is relevant to the proposed method because it shows that long-duration simulated microgravity can produce mixed touchscreen performance signals, with improved speed metrics alongside increased errors and longer touch durations, and that direct spaceflight kinematic data would be needed to distinguish conservative control from body mass underestimation.
