Time of measurement significantly affected all four indicators: throughput F(5, 1675.2)=45.16, p<0.001; movement time F(5, 1605.81)=72.465, p<0.001; error count chi-square(5)=15.10, p<0.01; touch duration F(5, 1631.26)=14.15, p<0.001. Throughput increased across the study, with estimated increases of about 0.49 bits/s relative to baseline and about 0.95 bits/s at 90 days after head-down tilt. Movement time decreased significantly from baseline through 21 days of head-down tilt and then remained stable. Error count was higher during head-down tilt than baseline, but baseline and recovery did not differ, and head-down tilt versus recovery contrasts were not significant. Touch duration was elevated during 5, 21, and 56 days of head-down tilt relative to baseline, while recovery did not differ from baseline; several recovery-versus-head-down-tilt contrasts were significant. The strength of the movement time versus index of difficulty relationship was highly similar in head-down tilt and non-head-down-tilt conditions, with a maximum R-squared difference of 0.01.
