[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Wochner et al., 2020-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5b80bce80ea49b57dd45a526f3666bd8f8940fe5)
- venue: Frontiers in Computational Neuroscience
- citation count: 21
[method]
The paper used a two-joint biophysical arm model with six Hill-type muscles to simulate free endpoint reaching toward a vertical target manifold. Static open-loop muscle stimulations, constrained to a six-dimensional unit hypercube, were selected by minimizing cost functions derived from different optimality principles. An additional external task constraint was added to enforce the desired horizontal endpoint at the bar. Because each candidate stimulation required a computationally expensive forward dynamic simulation and no analytical gradient was available, Bayesian optimization with a Gaussian process surrogate was used to search for low-cost stimulations in a sample-efficient way. This method is related to the proposed research method because both rely on a two-joint arm model and cost-based trajectory generation to predict reaching kinematics, although the paper addresses endpoint selection and optimality principles rather than actual or misperceived limb mass across movement directions.
[results]
Bayesian optimization produced lower total cost than random testing over 600 iterations in three runs per condition. The median total cost was significantly lower for Bayesian optimization, the interquartile ranges differed in magnitude, and a Student t-test rejected the null hypothesis of equal means at a significance level of 0.01. Across 30 repeated runs, the best observed cost decreased early in the optimization process and then stabilized near 8.09e-5, with the standard deviation narrowing as more iterations were performed.
[context]
The model contained six muscles with stimulation bounds from 0 to 1, a fixed starting posture with an elbow angle of 90 degrees and a shoulder angle of 0 degrees, zero initial velocities, and an initial muscle condition chosen to resemble a relaxed state. The target was a vertical bar representing a point-to-manifold endpoint located at 85% of total arm length. Optimization was episodic and simulation-based, with no closed-form expression for the trajectory as a function of stimulation. Evaluation used total cost, boxplot summaries, outlier thresholds based on 1.5 times the interquartile range, and hypothesis testing. The setup does not vary movement direction or limb mass, so its relevance to the proposed plan is methodological: it demonstrates that a two-joint musculoskeletal arm model can be used to generate and evaluate predicted reaching trajectories under task constraints.
[end related work 4]





