[method]
The paper used a touchscreen multidirectional tapping task implemented in the FittsTouch software. Each session consisted of 16 blocks, and each block contained 11 targets arranged in a circle, yielding 11 trials per block and 1056 trials per participant across six measurement points. Target distance and width were varied across 16 combinations, with distances of 294.88, 589.76, 884.64, and 1179.52 pixels and widths of 39.32, 78.63, 157.27, and 235.90 pixels, producing index-of-difficulty values from 1.17 to 4.95. Participants were instructed to tap the highlighted target as quickly and accurately as possible using their dominant hand; missed targets were marked red and had to be corrected before the next target appeared. Performance was quantified with movement time, throughput, error count or error rate, and touch duration. Data were aggregated by block after trial-level and block-level outlier removal, then analyzed with linear mixed models for continuous outcomes and a zero-inflated Poisson generalized linear mixed model for error count. Time of measurement was the fixed effect, and random intercepts were included for participant, target-distance-by-width item, and their interaction. Significant effects were followed up with Holm-adjusted pairwise contrasts. This method relates to the proposed in-flight foot-strap and tabletop-grasp method because both aim to control postural stability and keep the touchscreen task geometry consistent, but the paper achieved this in a head-down-tilt bed-rest analog rather than actual in-flight conditions and did not use foot straps or a tabletop.
[results]
Time of measurement significantly affected all four performance indicators: throughput F(5, 1675.2) = 45.16, p < 0.001; movement time F(5, 1605.81) = 72.465, p < 0.001; error count chi-square(5) = 15.10, p < 0.01; and touch duration F(5, 1631.26) = 14.15, p < 0.001. Throughput increased over the study period, with one later measurement showing an estimated increase of about 0.49 bits/s relative to baseline and the 90-day post-tilt measurement showing an estimated increase of almost 0.95 bits/s. Movement time decreased significantly from baseline through 21 days of head-down tilt and showed no further substantial change afterward. Error count was significantly higher during head-down-tilt conditions than at baseline, while baseline and recovery conditions did not differ significantly, and head-down-tilt versus recovery contrasts were not significant. Touch duration was significantly elevated at 5, 21, and 56 days of head-down tilt relative to baseline, was not different from baseline at 4 or 90 days after head-down tilt, and was generally lower in recovery than in head-down-tilt conditions, although this pattern did not hold for all pairwise comparisons. The strength of the movement-time versus index-of-difficulty relationship was highly similar in head-down-tilt and non-head-down-tilt conditions, with a maximum R-squared difference of 0.01.
[context]
The study included 24 healthy adults aged 24 to 55 years, with a mean age of 33.33 years and standard deviation of 9.38 years; 16 participants were male and 8 were female. Participants reported high familiarity with touch devices. They completed 60 days of head-down tilt at -6 degrees, with measurements taken 12 days before tilt, at 5, 21, and 56 days during tilt, and 4 and 90 days after tilt. The 4-day post-tilt measurement was available for only 12 participants because of data corruption. All task sessions were performed lying down, with the tablet placed on a stand on the bed, elbows supported on a pad, and the tablet positioned so participants could reach the screen with their dominant hand without lifting their head or elbow. The setup was photographed to reproduce the position across measurement points. This context is relevant to the proposed research plan because it provides a controlled touchscreen target-acquisition protocol under a microgravity analog, but it is not a direct test of the proposed in-flight foot-strap and left-hand tabletop stabilization method.
