[method]
The paper used a longitudinal within-subjects touchscreen target-acquisition method in a head-down-tilt microgravity analog. Participants performed a standardized multidirectional tapping task on a tablet: 16 blocks per session, each with 11 circularly arranged targets selected in a rotating opposite-target pattern; distance and width were varied across 16 combinations, yielding index of difficulty from 1.17 to 4.95. The task recorded touchscreen selection events, from which movement time, throughput, error count/error rate, and touch duration were computed. Data were aggregated by block after trial-level and block-level outlier removal, then analyzed in R with linear mixed-effects models for throughput, movement time, and touch duration, and a zero-inflated Poisson generalized linear mixed model for error count, with time of measurement as fixed effect and subject, item, and subject-by-item random intercepts. This method is related to the proposed plan because it evaluates touchscreen target acquisition in a microgravity analog, but it is not the proposed continuous 100 Hz finger-tracking method and does not extract reaction time, peak acceleration, peak speed, temporal symmetry, or submovements.
[results]
Time of measurement significantly affected all four indicators: throughput F(5, 1675.2)=45.16, p<0.001; movement time F(5, 1605.81)=72.465, p<0.001; error count chi-square(5)=15.10, p<0.01; touch duration F(5, 1631.26)=14.15, p<0.001. Throughput increased across the study, with estimated increases of about 0.49 bits/s relative to baseline and about 0.95 bits/s at 90 days post-head-down-tilt. Movement time decreased significantly from baseline through early head-down tilt, with significant contrasts involving baseline and head-down-tilt day 5 and no further significant changes after head-down-tilt day 21. Error count was significantly higher during head-down tilt than at baseline, while baseline and recovery did not differ significantly and head-down-tilt-versus-recovery contrasts were not significant. Touch duration was significantly elevated during head-down-tilt days 5, 21, and 56 relative to baseline; recovery did not differ from baseline, and recovery was generally lower than head-down tilt. The strength of the movement-time versus index-of-difficulty relationship was highly similar between head-down-tilt and non-head-down-tilt conditions, with a maximum R-squared difference of 0.01.
[context]
Twenty-four healthy adults aged 24 to 55 years (mean 33.33, SD 9.38; 16 male, 8 female) completed 60 days of -6 degree head-down tilt, with measurements 12 days before head-down tilt, on head-down-tilt days 5, 21, and 56, and 4 and 90 days after head-down tilt; day-4 post-head-down-tilt data were available for only 12 participants due to data corruption. Each participant completed one session per measurement point, totaling 1056 trials. The tablet was placed on a bed stand, participants lay with elbows on a pad, and used their dominant hand. The study was conducted in two successive cohorts of 12 participants. Outlier removal used more than three interquartile ranges from the median at trial level for movement time and touch duration, then retained only observations within two interquartile ranges from the median for aggregated movement time, touch duration, and throughput. Statistical tests used step-down familywise error-rate adjustment and alpha=0.05. The design had no ground-based control group, unlike the proposed plan, so practice effects may confound head-down-tilt-specific changes. Relative to the proposed method, this context shows a touchscreen microgravity-analog setting with repeated measurements and practice effects, but it lacks continuous finger-position sampling, directional effective-mass manipulation, and kinematic peak/submovement analyses needed to distinguish conservative control from body-mass underestimation.
