- in_text_citation: [(Elliott et al., unknown_d)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6a4899d4f0ae330dcc88b69ad3d76e7658c2aa5a)
- venue: Neuroscience and Biobehavioral Reviews
- citation count: 145
[method]
The paper used kinematic trajectory parsing to separate a primary distance-covering movement from corrective submovements. Speed and acceleration profiles were examined for a second acceleration peak or velocity reversal, which was treated as a corrective submovement. The analysis compared the limb's distance from the target at the end of the primary movement with final endpoint error, and quantified how often corrective submovements occurred, their amplitude, and their effect on movement time and accuracy. These corrective submovements were treated as feedback-based limb-target corrections, distinct from continuous impulse control, and were used to test whether late trajectory adjustments reduced error. This is directly related to the proposed method because both approaches use submovement decomposition of speed profiles to distinguish feedforward primary movement from feedback-based corrective actions; in the proposed microgravity plan, secondary submovements would be used to test whether mass underestimation produces compensatory feedback corrections.
[results]
Corrective submovements were associated with further reduction of endpoint error after the primary movement. In a practice study of rapid aiming to a high-difficulty target, early performance showed primary endpoints that undershot and hit the target equally often, with target overshoots on about 5% of trials. After four days of practice, movement time decreased, primary endpoint variability decreased, primary endpoints landed on target on about 70% of trials, undershoots decreased to about 25%, and overshoots remained about 5%, indicating fewer and smaller corrective submovements. In vision and no-vision aiming, participants were closer to the target at the end of the primary movement when vision was available, and when a corrective submovement occurred it further reduced error; no-vision trials had larger endpoint error and variability but still showed corrective limb-target regulation. In target-shift work, a corrective submovement toward the new target position took about 250 to 290 ms to complete, while the perturbation added only about 100 ms to overall movement time.
[context]
The relevant evidence comes from adult rapid manual aiming and reaching experiments using small targets, controlled movement amplitudes, and manipulations of vision, target size or position, effector mass, gravity, and practice. Corrective submovements were identified from kinematic profiles rather than from muscle activity or force measurements, and the main outcomes were endpoint error, primary movement endpoint distribution, movement time, and submovement frequency or amplitude. In the proposed research plan, the analogous decomposition will be applied to 100 Hz finger position data from taikonauts making 12 cm reaches to three directions under a 650 ms completion constraint, where the presence, frequency, and timing of secondary submovements will be interpreted as feedback-based corrections that may reflect body mass underestimation in microgravity.
[end related work 1]

