- in_text_citation: [(Bieg et al., 2023-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f3ff0bedae6143e46d2dcaca72405a0669643591)
- venue: CEAS Space Journal
- citation count: 1
[method]
The paper used a within-subjects 60-day head-down tilt microgravity analog design in which 24 healthy adults completed a standardized multidirectional touchscreen tapping task at six repeated measurement phases. The task required participants to select highlighted targets on a tablet as quickly and accurately as possible using the dominant hand. Each session consisted of 16 blocks, and each block contained 11 targets arranged in a circle and selected in a rotating opposite-target order, producing 1056 trials per participant across the full study. Target distance and width were varied across 16 combinations, yielding index of difficulty values from 1.17 to 4.95 bits. Performance was summarized by block for throughput, movement time, error count, and touch duration. The primary statistical approach used linear mixed-effects models for throughput, movement time, and touch duration, and a zero-inflated Poisson generalized linear mixed model for error count. Measurement phase was entered as a categorical fixed effect, and random intercepts were included for participant, target difficulty item, and their interaction. Pairwise contrasts with Holm correction were used when phase was significant. This method relates to the proposed research plan because it tests phase-related changes in touchscreen target acquisition performance, but it differs from the proposed repeated-measures ANOVA with movement direction because it did not model movement direction as a statistical factor and did not analyze peak acceleration, peak speed timing, or submovement decomposition.
[results]
Measurement phase significantly affected all four performance indicators: throughput F(5, 1675.2) = 45.16, p < 0.001; movement time F(5, 1605.81) = 72.465, p < 0.001; error count chi-square(5) = 15.10, p < 0.01; and touch duration F(5, 1631.26) = 14.15, p < 0.001. Throughput increased over the study period, with estimated increases of about 0.49 bits/s and nearly 0.95 bits/s relative to baseline at later measurement points, and some recovery conditions showed higher throughput than head-down tilt conditions. Movement time decreased significantly from baseline and early head-down tilt through day 21 of head-down tilt, with no significant further change after day 21. Error count was significantly higher during head-down tilt conditions than at baseline, but baseline, recovery day 4, and recovery day 90 did not differ significantly, and head-down tilt versus recovery contrasts were not significant. Touch duration was significantly elevated at head-down tilt days 5, 21, and 56 compared with baseline, while recovery day 4 and recovery day 90 did not differ from baseline; several head-down tilt versus recovery contrasts were significant. The strength of the target difficulty to movement time relationship was highly similar between head-down tilt and non-head-down tilt conditions, with a maximum R-squared difference of 0.01.
[context]
The study was conducted as part of a 60-day head-down tilt bed-rest protocol at the German Aerospace Center in Cologne, Germany, with 24 healthy participants aged 24 to 55 years, mean age 33.33 years, 16 male and 8 female, and high self-reported touch device familiarity. Measurements were taken while participants were lying down: baseline 12 days before head-down tilt, head-down tilt days 5, 21, and 56, recovery day 4, and recovery day 90. Recovery day 4 data were available for only 12 participants due to data corruption. The tablet was mounted on a bed stand, elbows were supported, and the tablet position was photographed and reproduced across sessions. Outliers were removed at the trial level for movement time and touch duration when values exceeded three interquartile ranges from the median, and aggregated block-level values were retained only if within two interquartile ranges from the median for movement time, touch duration, and throughput. Error rate was transformed into error count bounded between 0 and 11. The design is a microgravity analog rather than actual spaceflight, and the task is a multidirectional tapping task rather than the proposed rapid reaching task with three fixed directions, a 650 ms time constraint, effective-mass manipulation, and submovement decomposition.
