[method]
The paper used a separate healthy ground-control cohort of 12 subjects who completed the same MRI and sensorimotor assessments at four time points over 90 days (days 1, 7.5, 43.5, and 83.5) while 18 head-down bed rest subjects underwent MRI at seven time points and behavioral testing before and after bed rest. This is methodologically related to the proposed age-matched ground control group for taikonauts because it provides a non-spaceflight repeated-measurement baseline to separate bed-rest/spaceflight-analog effects from practice and random variation. The paper did not explicitly state that the controls were age-matched, and the control time points did not align with the bed-rest time points. The control data were incorporated into linear mixed models that compared bed-rest and control slopes, modeled random subject intercepts, included time as a continuous or factor variable, included age as a covariate, adjusted for exercise group and group-by-time interactions, used restricted maximum likelihood, and applied Bonferroni correction. For behavioral outcomes, the first time points were omitted to account for practice effects, and pre-specified contrasts tested a stable baseline, bed-rest-related change, and post-bed-rest recovery.
[results]
The provided excerpt does not report the primary quantitative bed-rest-versus-control outcomes for gray matter volume or sensorimotor performance. It reports that the control group was used to frame the repeated-measurement comparisons. In the reported brain-behavioral analyses, regional gray matter volume changes in vestibular cortex (b = 6306.72, t(16) = 2.69, p = .017) and cerebellar lobule V (b = 743.71, t(16) = 2.90, p = .011) were associated with balance performance changes, but these associations did not survive multiple-comparison correction. Voxelwise analysis found that focal gray matter increases from the last pre-bed-rest to the last in-bed-rest time point were associated with SOT-5 balance changes, with a peak-voxel Pearson correlation of .76 in bilateral precuneus and pre-/postcentral gyrus, family-wise error corrected p < .05, but the result did not survive an additional Bonferroni correction for four tests. No associations were found between focal gray matter changes and Functional Mobility Test performance.
[context]
The study population comprised 18 head-down bed rest subjects randomized to exercise and non-exercise groups and 12 healthy controls. Bed rest lasted 70 days at 6 degrees head-down tilt in a NASA bed-rest facility. MRI was collected at pre-bed-rest days -12.5 and -7, during bed rest days 8.5, 50.5, and 66, and post-bed-rest days +7 and +12; sensorimotor measures were collected at pre-bed-rest days -12.5 and -7 and post-bed-rest days +0, +7, and +12. Controls were measured at days 1, 7.5, 43.5, and 83.5 over 90 days, and their timeline was optimized for another study. Behavioral instruments included EquiTest SOT-5 and SOT-5M and an in-house Functional Mobility Test; SOT trials were 20 seconds, repeated three times, on a sway-referenced surface, with SOT-5M adding cued head pitch of ±20 degrees at 0.33 Hz. Imaging preprocessing used FSL, VBM8, SPM, BRAINS, N4ITK bias correction, and BET masking with a fractional intensity threshold of 0.15. Voxelwise permutation analysis used 15,000 permutations, variance smoothing equivalent to 8 mm full-width at half-maximum, masking to significant gray matter change regions, adjustment for intracranial volume, and family-wise error correction at p < .05. The paper's design is relevant to the proposed ground-control method but differs in that it used a head-down bed-rest analog rather than actual spaceflight, did not explicitly report age matching, and assessed balance and mobility rather than reaching kinematics or anisotropic limb inertia.
