- in_text_citation: [(Kim, and Hyun, 2023-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/159667e42ba7bc3ae9b990a18e6dd5e768ef1d41)
- venue: PLoS ONE
- citation count: 4
[method]
The paper used a repeated-session touchscreen pointing task to evaluate a haptic joystick device and magnetic snap assistance. Participants moved a 20 mm cursor to a 20 mm target on a touchscreen laptop and pressed a key when the cursor center entered the target. The initial cursor-target distance was 130 mm from the screen center, target and cursor positions were randomly defined, and the snap selectable area was set to 35 px, approximately 5 mm. Six conditions were tested: touchscreen without snap, touchscreen with software snap, haptic joystick without snap, haptic joystick with software snap, haptic joystick with hardware snap, and haptic joystick with both hardware and software snap. Each condition included five sessions of ten trials, with condition order randomized. This is related to the proposed consistent tablet placement method because it involves a touchscreen pointing/reaching task with fixed task geometry across repeated sessions, although the paper did not explicitly report tablet placement or device positioning.
[results]
Task completion time improved across sessions, but no statistically significant difference was found between the fourth and fifth sessions, so sessions four and five were combined for analysis. The touchscreen no-snap condition was slowest, with a mean completion time of 5578.9 ms and SD of 2716.0 ms. Touchscreen with software snap was faster at 4898.6 ms, SD 1989.6 ms, and significantly faster than no snap, p < 0.05. For the haptic joystick, no snap took 5246.4 ms, SD 2791.7 ms, while software snap took 4304.0 ms, SD 2045.2 ms, and was significantly faster, p < 0.01. Hardware snap alone took 4817.3 ms, SD 3728.7 ms, and was not significantly different from software snap, p = 0.979. The combined hardware and software snap condition was fastest at 3768.8 ms, SD 2064.2 ms, and was significantly faster than all other conditions, all p < 0.05. NASA TLX workload followed the same pattern: combined snap had the lowest workload and touchscreen no-snap had the highest workload, both with all p < 0.001; haptic joystick no-snap and touchscreen software snap were similar, p = 1.0, and haptic joystick hardware snap and haptic joystick software snap were similar, p = 1.0.
[context]
Twelve participants took part, with mean age 27.67 years and SD 4.46 years, including five females and seven males. Each participant completed about 1 hour and 30 minutes across all conditions. The experiment used a Dell Precision 5530 2-in-1 touchscreen laptop. The haptic device was a small solenoid-magnet joystick approximately 26 mm by 26 mm in diameter and 12 mm in height, with a two-dimensional movement range of about plus or minus 6 mm. Measured maximum forces were approximately 3.26 N for pushing and 2.73 N for pulling. The task geometry was kept fixed in terms of cursor size, target size, initial distance, and snap threshold, which is analogous to the proposed method of maintaining consistent tablet placement to preserve task geometry, but the paper did not explicitly control or report tablet placement itself.
