- in_text_citation: [(Bieg et al., 2023-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f3ff0bedae6143e46d2dcaca72405a0669643591)
- venue: CEAS Space Journal
- citation count: 1
[method]
The paper used a fixed tablet placement procedure as a within-subject control for a touchscreen target-acquisition task. At the baseline measurement, the tablet was mounted on a stand on the participant's bed, the participant's elbows were placed on a pad, and the tablet position was adjusted so the participant could see the screen without lifting the head and could reach all screen areas with the dominant hand without lifting the elbow. The position was photographed and then reproduced as accurately as possible at later measurement points. This maintained the same screen-to-body geometry across repeated sessions during head-down tilt and recovery. The paper does not report a numeric check of placement error, so the consistency was procedural rather than directly verified. This is directly related to the proposed method, which specifies that the tablet will be positioned consistently across all sessions to maintain experimental conditions, and to the research plan's need to keep target geometry stable while taikonauts perform directional touchscreen reaches.
[results]
Using this placement control, the study obtained comparable touchscreen tapping performance across six measurement points. Significant time-of-measurement effects were found for all indicators: throughput F(5, 1675.2) = 45.16, p < 0.001; movement time F(5, 1605.81) = 72.465, p < 0.001; error count chi-square(5) = 15.10, p < 0.01; touch duration F(5, 1631.26) = 14.15, p < 0.001. Throughput increased over the study, with estimated increases of 0.49 bits/s relative to baseline and about 0.95 bits/s at 90 days after head-down tilt. Movement time decreased significantly from baseline through 21 days of head-down tilt and then remained relatively stable. Error count was significantly higher during head-down tilt than at baseline, while recovery values did not differ significantly from baseline. Touch duration was elevated during the 5-, 21-, and 56-day head-down tilt measurements relative to baseline. The strength of the target-acquisition relationship was highly similar between head-down tilt and non-head-down tilt conditions, with a maximum difference in R-squared of 0.01.
[context]
The study involved 24 healthy adults aged 24 to 55 years, 16 male and 8 female, who completed 60 days of head-down tilt at -6 degrees with measurements 12 days before, 5, 21, and 56 days during, and 4 and 90 days after tilt; the 4-day recovery measurement was available for only 12 participants due to data corruption. Participants lay on the bed and used their dominant hand to tap 11 targets arranged in a circle across 16 blocks per session, for 1056 trials per participant. Target distances were 294.88, 589.76, 884.64, and 1179.52 pixels and widths were 39.32, 78.63, 157.27, and 235.90 pixels, producing index of difficulty values from 1.17 to 4.95. The task was implemented in FittsTouch on a tablet. This context differs from the proposed plan, which uses 12 taikonauts, three 12-cm targets at 45, 90, and 135 degrees, 120 trials per session, 100 Hz finger tracking, foot straps, and left-hand tabletop stabilization, but shares the core requirement of stable tablet placement to preserve task geometry across repeated spaceflight or analog sessions.
[end related work 2]

