  "contradictions": "The strongest contradiction is methodological: [(Thonnard et al., 2020-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/576fedf707528768a0d83b8bec20672d07f495ea) found that pelvis restraint was strongly recommended and shoulder straps were important for stability during vertical and horizontal oscillations and vertical collisions, whereas the proposed method relies only on foot straps and left-hand tabletop stabilization. If parasitic body movements are not constrained, the plan's 100-Hz finger kinematics, peak acceleration timing, and submovement decomposition may be contaminated by trunk or shoulder motion, weakening the dissociation between conservative control and mass underestimation. The next strongest contradiction is that [(Bieg et al., 2023-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f3ff0bedae6143e46d2dcaca72405a0669643591) shows repeated touchscreen measurements across simulated microgravity produce mixed signals, with speed-based throughput improving over time likely due to practice while error count and touch duration increase, and no clear early post-head-down-tilt drop at recovery day 4 or day 90. This contradicts the assumption that post-flight sessions will cleanly reveal a persistent microgravity-related slowing or recovery, and it suggests that a 12-taikonaut repeated-measures ANOVA may be vulnerable to practice, missing data, and non-sphericity, especially because Bieg et al. used linear mixed effects models and a zero-inflated Poisson model and had missing recovery-day 4 data for 12 of 24 participants. The weakest contradiction is that [(Macaluso et al., 2017-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b3cfc02cac9b750070c5469e65b890259c2934ac), despite using the same tablet reaching paradigm, does not provide quantitative effect sizes, p-values, participant numbers, target geometry, sampling rate, or statistical details, so it cannot confirm that the proposed 12-cm targets, 45/90/135-degree anisotropic directions, 120 trials, 650-ms constraint, or 100-Hz sampling will yield sufficient signal for submovement decomposition and model-based mass tests.",
  "suggested_action": "Most important, strengthen the in-flight restraint and stability validation by adding pelvis or torso restraint and shoulder straps where feasible, and record trunk, shoulder, and hip displacement as in [(Macaluso et al., 2017-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b3cfc02cac9b750070c5469e65b890259c2934ac), so that trials with excessive body motion can be excluded or modeled as covariates. Second, replace or supplement repeated-measures ANOVA with linear mixed-effects models and a zero-inflated count model for errors, following [(Bieg et al., 2023-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f3ff0bedae6143e46d2dcaca72405a0669643591), with random intercepts for participant and target or difficulty item, and pre-register handling of missing sessions; also conduct a power analysis for 12 taikonauts, 3 directions, and 3 phases, and consider adding practice blocks and counterbalanced target order to separate practice from physiological effects. Third, extend post-flight sampling beyond immediate recovery, because [(Bieg et al., 2023-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f3ff0bedae6143e46d2dcaca72405a0669643591) did not detect an early drop at day 4 or day 90, and include longer recovery visits if mission constraints allow. Fourth, add speed-accuracy metrics beyond movement duration, such as error count, touch duration, success rate, and throughput or index of difficulty, to capture the mixed performance pattern reported in [(Bieg et al., 2023-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f3ff0bedae6143e46d2dcaca72405a0669643591). Finally, validate the two-joint arm model, movement utility theory, forward optimal controller, and submovement decomposition on the age-matched ground control data before in-flight use, and report smoothing, 100-Hz temporal resolution, and submovement detection thresholds to ensure that peak acceleration timing and corrective submovements are not artifacts.",
