  "contradictions": "The strongest contradiction is that the more detailed 2017-08 design, which extends the 2017-06 ground-control concept, did not explicitly state that the 12 healthy controls were age-matched to the 18 head-down bed rest participants, and their measurement time points (days 1, 7.5, 43.5, and 83.5 over 90 days) did not align with the bed-rest MRI and behavioral time points. This contradicts the proposed method's assumption that an age-matched ground control group alone will adequately control repeated-measurement confounding; session timing, number of sessions, task exposure, and practice effects also need to be matched. A second contradiction is that the same study used a relatively small control group (n=12) and reported brain-behavior associations that did not survive multiple-comparison correction, such as vestibular cortex b=6306.72, t(16)=2.69, p=.017 and cerebellar lobule V b=743.71, t(16)=2.90, p=.011, and a voxelwise peak-voxel Pearson correlation of .76 that survived FWE p<.05 but not an additional Bonferroni correction for four tests; the voxelwise analysis also used 15,000 permutations, 8 mm FWHM variance smoothing, and intracranial volume adjustment. This suggests that ground-control comparisons in small repeated-measurement studies may be underpowered and vulnerable to false-positive or unstable effects, which is relevant to the proposed 12-taikonaut design and submovement frequency analyses. A third contradiction is domain mismatch: both related works assessed HDBR-related balance, mobility, and MRI outcomes, not rapid touchscreen reaching, anisotropic limb inertia, peak acceleration/speed timing, or submovement decomposition, so they do not directly support the specific kinematic hypotheses in the research plan. The weakest contradiction is that [(Koppelmans et al., 2017-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/bc9308a6e60979355fa1ced992d080de4438a1f2) provides no quantitative results or detailed reproducibility information in the supplied excerpt, limiting its ability to validate the proposed control-group approach.",
  "suggested_action": "The most important improvement is to specify the ground control group as a repeated-measurement control that matches the taikonaut protocol in session number, session timing, task, and exposure, not only age. Controls should perform the same right-index-finger tablet reaching task with the same 12 cm targets at 45 degrees, 90 degrees, and 135 degrees, the same 120 trials per session, the same pseudorandom order, the same 650 ms time constraint, the same auditory beep in half trials, the same 100 Hz position recording, and the same tabletop stabilization and foot support as feasible on the ground. The second most important action is to replace or supplement the planned repeated-measures ANOVA with linear mixed models that include group, phase, direction, group-by-phase, group-by-direction, and group-by-phase-by-direction, random intercepts and random slopes for participants, baseline covariates such as age, sex, height, mass, handedness, and baseline movement duration, and appropriate multiple-comparison correction for peak acceleration, peak speed, their timing, and submovement frequency. The third action is to predefine a power analysis or minimum control-group size, ideally at least comparable to the 12 taikonauts and preferably larger, to detect group-by-phase interactions on the primary kinematic outcomes, because [(Koppelmans et al., 2017-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0b8f60cb14f6bd8139a2d02dcf8c7110f53941d3) showed that a 12-subject control group can yield non-robust corrected results. The fourth action is to explicitly model practice effects by including initial practice trials or first-session data as a covariate, omitting or modeling early trials as in [(Koppelmans et al., 2017-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0b8f60cb14f6bd8139a2d02dcf8c7110f53941d3), and using the control group to estimate expected practice-related changes in planned movement duration, peak kinematics, and submovement frequency before interpreting in-flight changes, and to calibrate movement utility theory and forward optimal controller baseline parameters for planned duration and effective mass perception.",
  "soundness_score": 6
}
