  "support": "The strongest support comes from the newer space-analog study by [(Bieg et al., 2023-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f3ff0bedae6143e46d2dcaca72405a0669643591), which directly used a fixed tablet placement procedure as a within-subject control for repeated touchscreen target acquisition during 60 days of -6° head-down tilt. The tablet was mounted on a stand on the participant's bed, elbows were placed on a pad, the screen was adjusted so participants could see it without lifting the head and reach all screen areas without lifting the elbow, and the position was photographed and reproduced at later measurement points. With 24 healthy adults (24-55 years, 16 male, 8 female), 11 circular targets, 16 blocks, 1056 trials per participant, target distances of 294.88, 589.76, 884.64, and 1179.52 px, target widths of 39.32, 78.63, 157.27, and 235.90 px, and index of difficulty 1.17-4.95 in FittsTouch, the study obtained comparable tapping performance across six measurement points and a highly stable Fitts relationship, with maximum R-squared difference of 0.01 between head-down tilt and non-head-down tilt conditions. Significant time-of-measurement effects (throughput F(5, 1675.2)=45.16, p<0.001; movement time F(5, 1605.81)=72.465, p<0.001; error count chi-square(5)=15.10, p<0.01; touch duration F(5, 1631.26)=14.15, p<0.001) show that stable placement can preserve task geometry well enough to reveal phase-specific changes rather than placement artifacts. This directly supports the proposed plan's statement that the tablet will be positioned consistently across all sessions for 12 taikonauts performing directional touchscreen reaches, because it demonstrates that repeated spaceflight-analog sessions can be made comparable when screen-to-body geometry is controlled. As an iterative sequence, the older lab study by [(Kim, and Hyun, 2023-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/159667e42ba7bc3ae9b990a18e6dd5e768ef1d41) provides weaker but complementary support: it used a repeated-session touchscreen pointing task with fixed task geometry (20 mm cursor and target, 130 mm initial cursor-target distance, 35 px snap area, six conditions, five sessions of ten trials, randomized order) and showed learning stabilized by sessions four and five, with condition effects such as combined hardware/software snap fastest at 3768.8 ms (SD 2064.2) and lowest NASA-TLX workload. Although it did not explicitly control or report tablet placement, its fixed geometry and repeated-session design support the broader principle that stable task geometry is needed to interpret repeated touchscreen reaching data, and the newer Bieg study extends that principle to a spaceflight analog by adding explicit tablet placement control.",
  "contradictions": "The most important contradiction is that the same newer space-analog evidence that supports consistent placement also shows the proposed method is under-specified if it relies only on procedural consistency. [(Bieg et al., 2023-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f3ff0bedae6143e46d2dcaca72405a0669643591) reported that the tablet position was photographed and reproduced as accurately as possible, but did not report a numeric check of placement error, so consistency was procedural rather than directly verified. In the proposed plan, where targets are 12 cm away at 45°, 90°, and 135°, movement duration is constrained to 650 ms, finger position is sampled at 100 Hz, and submovement decomposition is used to infer feedforward versus feedback control, even small unmeasured shifts in tablet position, tilt, or screen-to-elbow geometry could change effective target distance, movement direction, and perceived limb mass, thereby confounding the repeated-measures ANOVA factors of phase and direction. The significant time-of-measurement effects in [(Bieg et al., 2023-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f3ff0bedae6143e46d2dcaca72405a0669643591), including higher error counts during head-down tilt and elevated touch duration at 5, 21, and 56 days, also caution that consistent tablet placement is necessary but not sufficient to maintain identical experimental conditions; posture, head-down tilt, and recovery can still alter performance. A weaker contradiction is that the older study by [(Kim, and Hyun, 2023-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/159667e42ba7bc3ae9b990a18e6dd5e768ef1d41) did not explicitly report tablet placement or device positioning, so its fixed-geometry touchscreen pointing task cannot be taken as direct evidence that tablet placement itself was controlled. Its setup also differed from the proposed plan: it used a Dell Precision 5530 2-in-1 laptop, a key-press completion criterion, haptic joystick conditions, snap assistance, 12 participants, and 50 trials per condition, rather than 12 taikonauts, 120 trials per session, 100 Hz finger tracking, foot straps, left-hand tabletop stabilization, and three 12-cm directional targets. Thus, the newer Bieg work both supports and qualifies the older Kim fixed-geometry approach: it shows the principle is relevant to space analogs, but it also reveals that procedural consistency without numeric verification is a methodological gap.",
  "suggested_action": "The most important improvement is to add objective, quantified tablet-placement verification to the proposed method. The plan should specify fiducial markers or a camera-based calibration procedure to record tablet position, tilt, and screen-to-body geometry relative to the participant's torso, elbow, shoulder, and head, and should report placement error for each session with a predefined acceptance threshold, for example less than 1-2 mm in screen coordinates or less than 1 degree in tilt, before data are accepted. This directly addresses the main limitation in [(Bieg et al., 2023-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f3ff0bedae6143e46d2dcaca72405a0669643591), where placement was photographed and reproduced but not numerically verified, and it protects the proposed 12-cm target distances at 45°, 90°, and 135° from unmeasured drift. Second, the method should fully calibrate and report the task geometry: tablet resolution/DPI, physical target size, start position, target coordinates in both pixels and centimeters, coordinate system, 100 Hz finger tracking, 650 ms time constraint, and the left-hand tabletop stabilization and foot-strap setup, ideally using a validated touchscreen task platform such as FittsTouch or an equivalent implementation. This would make the proposed directional reaching task comparable to the Bieg space-analog protocol and to the fixed-geometry repeated-session design in [(Kim, and Hyun, 2023-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/159667e42ba7bc3ae9b990a18e6dd5e768ef1d41). Third, because [(Bieg et al., 2023-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f3ff0bedae6143e46d2dcaca72405a0669643591) used 1056 trials per participant across 16 blocks while the proposed plan uses only 120 trials per session, the plan should include a power analysis or increase trials/blocks, especially for submovement decomposition and repeated-measures ANOVA with phase and direction; if placement error is recorded, it should be included as a covariate or used to exclude sessions that exceed the threshold. Finally, a pilot placement protocol with the age-matched ground control group should be used to standardize tablet mounting, elbow support, head position, and reachability, analogous to the Bieg procedure, so that in-flight sessions can be reproduced with minimal operator variability.",
